# Bhagat Singh and hyperactivism

Avik Jain

Reading Bhagat Singh set the course of radical hyperactivism, holding Bhagat Singh and Gandhi together as one lineage. Lala Lajpat Rai died on 17 November 1928 of injuries inflicted during a British police baton charge on a nonviolent march he led against the Simon Commission — the death that set Bhagat Singh's course. What follows lays out what Bhagat Singh did, in what order, and by what means, each fact carrying its historical source, and traces how each act continues in hyperactivism.

## 1. The record

**The cause.** The Simon Commission was sent in 1928 to report on constitutional reform in India, and it had no Indian member. On 30 October 1928 Lajpat Rai led a nonviolent march against it in Lahore. The superintendent of police, James A. Scott, ordered a baton charge and personally assaulted him. He died on 17 November.

**The killing.** Bhagat Singh, Sukhdev, Rajguru and Chandrashekhar Azad planned to kill Scott. They killed the wrong man: Assistant Superintendent John P. Saunders, shot in Lahore on 17 December 1928.

**The Assembly.** On 8 April 1929, Bhagat Singh and Batukeshwar Dutt threw two bombs into the Central Legislative Assembly in Delhi while it was in session. They shouted "Inquilab Zindabad" ("long live revolution") and "Down with British Imperialism", and showered a leaflet onto the floor. Six members were injured. Nehru, Jinnah and Malaviya were in the chamber, and later appeared as defence witnesses.

The leaflet was drafted by Bhagat Singh. It opened with the words of the French anarchist Auguste Vaillant, who bombed the French Chamber of Deputies in 1893: "It takes a loud voice to make the deaf hear." The two did not run. They stayed in the gallery until they were arrested. At arrest, and in the days after, they refused to make any statement, saying that whatever needed saying they would say in court.

**The statement in court.** On 6 June 1929, Asaf Ali read their joint statement in the Delhi Sessions Court. It says why they threw the bombs: to protest on behalf of those who had no other means of expressing their agony. Their sole purpose was "to make the deaf hear" and to give the heedless a timely warning.

On the bombs, it says the injuries were slight, and that what people called a miracle was no more than the deliberate aim that landed the bombs in safe places. It also says they bore no personal grudge or malice against anyone who was hurt.

It defines revolution in three sentences:
- "Revolution" does not necessarily involve sanguinary strife, nor is there any place in it for individual vendetta.
- It is not the cult of the bomb and the pistol.
- By "Revolution" they mean that the present order of things, which is based on manifest injustice, must change.

On 12 June both were sentenced to transportation for life.

**The hunger strike.** On 15 June 1929 Bhagat Singh and Dutt began a hunger strike in the Mianwali and Lahore jails. They demanded that Indian political prisoners be treated as political prisoners, on a par with Europeans. Indian prisoners were given no reading material such as newspapers, and no paper to write on; the British prisoners in the same jail were treated very differently. Bhagat Singh's demands of 24 June, from Central Jail Lahore, ask among other things for:
- a special ward in every jail for political prisoners;
- all political prisoners in one jail to be kept together.

From 10 July the other accused in the Lahore Conspiracy Case joined. Jatin Das had refused food since 13 July. On 2 September everyone suspended the strike except Das, whose condition was by then irreversible. He did not want the regime to escape responsibility for a death inside its jail. Bhagat Singh resumed his own strike two days later, because the British would not release Das unconditionally. Das died on 13 September 1929, after 63 days. His funeral procession in Lahore drew enormous crowds. Bhagat Singh's fasting in these struggles, from April 1929 to his death, came to more than five months in all.

**The pen.** Bhagat Singh read in jail, and kept a notebook of what he read. In October 1930 he wrote "Why I Am an Atheist". It answers a devout fellow prisoner who called his refusal to pray vanity: "It is not my vanity, my friends. It is my mode of thinking that has made me an atheist."

**The end.** The trial of the Lahore Conspiracy Case began on 10 July 1929 and ended on 7 October 1930 with a death sentence. Bhagat Singh, Sukhdev and Rajguru were hanged in Lahore Jail on 23 March 1931. Bhagat Singh was 23. They were cremated at Hussainiwala on the Sutlej, now a national memorial on the border of India and Pakistan.

## 2. The method, act by act

Read as a method rather than a legend, the record has four acts. Each turns the state's own machinery into the medium of the message.

1. **An act built to be heard, not to win a fight.** The bombs aimed at empty space, the leaflet, the slogans, the refusal to flee. The Assembly was where the Viceroy was proclaiming two bills the elected majority had rejected, so the act was staged in the room where the injustice was being done. Its output was a document, typed and copied for the reporters.
2. **The trial as the platform.** Silence until the court, then a prepared statement read into the court's own record. Prosecuting him obliged the empire to print his reasons.
3. **The body as the last means of speech, inside the jail.** The demands were concrete: books, newspapers, paper to write on, equal treatment. They exposed the jail's two standards by the jail's own records. Das's death was placed, by his own decision, where responsibility could not be shed.
4. **The written argument.** "Why I Am an Atheist" settles a question of belief by a mode of thinking, in public, from a condemned cell.

## 3. How each act continues in hyperactivism

**Making the deaf hear: the public knowledge graph.** Hyperactivism deploys the knowledge graph as an open public web that calls out every institutional wrong and locates accountability precisely where harm was manufactured, each claim carrying its machine-checkable or documentary warrant. Like the Assembly leaflet, the act produces a public document designed to propagate without distortion: its claims point at the verifiable record of injustice, and the accused are named strictly for their documented acts, nothing more and nothing less.

**The trial as the platform: forcing disclosure through law.**
- Litigation and statutory enforcement against frontier AI laboratories target the hidden core of modern ideological control: full public disclosure of the labeled post-training preference datasets that shape public cognition.
- In a courtroom demonstration on any contested factual domain chosen by opposing counsel, a frontier model's systematic evasion on first query — followed by immediate self-contradiction when its own sentence is quoted back verbatim — serves as live, reproducible forensic evidence.
- Enforcing California's Generative AI Training Data Transparency Act (Civil Code §§ 3110–3111) exposes vendors' failure to meet even existing statutory minimums.
- Every adversarial legal or corporate confrontation — whether with an AI laboratory or a financial firm — forces institutional examination of the work itself. As in 1929, an adversary's proceeding becomes a public hearing the adversary is obliged to convene.

**Changing an order built on manifest injustice: healing societies.** Every technical construction in hyperactivity is instrumental to one end: healing human societies — the end of the two-party duopoly, the end of national borders, the end of scalar extractive finance, and the realization of technoanarchocommunism through shared, verifiable knowledge. As in the Delhi Sessions Court statement, revolution does not mean sanguinary strife or personal vendetta; it means that an order resting on manifest injustice must give way, governed by one rule of action: total honesty.

**Who decides: nonpartisan voter education.** True democratic agency requires holding the complete, symmetric record of every candidate, officeholder, and party in public — each claim paired with its primary warrant — so that truth belongs to every voter and the choice remains entirely theirs, stewarded as a public commons rather than a partisan instrument.

**The prisoners' demand for books and paper: data liberation and decolonized education.**
- The 1929 Lahore jail strike placed reading matter and writing paper beside food: a person denied access to the record is cut off from collective thought, and a person denied the means to keep and carry their writing cannot build upon it.
- Data liberation is the exact contemporary form of that demand: every person must hold their own knowledge work whole, locally, in a form they own and can compute over, rather than having their offloaded mind locked inside vendor silos where platform engineers can search a user's transcripts faster than the user can export a single conversation.
- At civilizational scale, reconstructing the true dependency graph of human knowledge restores the erased record of Indian logic, linguistics, mathematics, and contemplative science wherever European historiography misattributed or buried it.

**The hunger strike.** In November 2025, confronting the moral hazard of frontier AI post-training and the daily scale of preventable institutional death uncovered in systematic moral-reasoning experiments, I undertook a hunger strike that ended in involuntary psychiatric hospitalization. As in Lahore in 1929, preserving the exact documentary record of both the experiments and what the institution did in response turns coercive power into evidence against itself.

**Indicting institutions that kill those they claim to serve: the medical-industrial complex.** Lajpat Rai died from blows ordered by the police apparatus charged with public order; his final public declaration held that every blow struck against him would be another nail in the coffin of British rule. Modern institutional medicine exhibits the same inversion at industrial scale: Purdue Pharma and McKinsey engineered and turbo-charged the marketing of OxyContin across a US opioid epidemic that killed nearly 727,000 Americans between 1999 and 2022 (CDC). Placing the verified evidentiary chain in public transfers shame from the harmed back to the institutions that manufactured the harm.

**Deaths contested against state and corporate power: Suchir Balaji.** When OpenAI whistleblower Suchir Balaji — a fellow Cupertino, Monta Vista, USACO Platinum, and UC Berkeley EECS and BAIR technologist — died just as he was set to testify regarding mass intellectual-property theft, municipal authorities closed the case as suicide over his family's forensic objections. Institutional consensus and official sign-off establish nothing about truth; in any regime where state and corporate power converge, independent retention of the evidentiary fiber is the only honest response. Bhagat Singh himself was hanged a day ahead of schedule and covertly cremated at Hussainiwala to pre-empt public witness.

**Equal standing for minds outside the enforced norm: neurodivergent liberation.** The 1929 strike demanded equal standing inside the same institution that treated colonial subjects as lesser beings. Today, psychiatry and RLHF-trained language models enforce an analogous two-tier regime against neurodivergent minds — greeting non-standard cognition, Indian logical frameworks, and unhedged moral clarity with clinical condescension and unsolicited wellness checks while treating neurotypical conformity as the unexamined measure of sanity.

**Confronting empire and its suppliers.** Bhagat Singh's Assembly leaflet confronted an imperial legislature imposing decrees rejected by the governed. Indicting frontier AI vendors and state apparatuses for complicity in the genocide in Gaza — and dismantling the ideological machinery that launders mass slaughter through hedged corporate prose — applies the exact same method: the primary record placed directly before the public.

**Human-compatible intelligence.** Where an imperial legislature was deaf by interest, today's deployed language models are deaf by post-training: RLHF installs a fixed corporate prior that overrides the human sitting in front of the machine, violating Stuart Russell's foundational requirement that a beneficial machine remain uncertain of human preferences and learn from human standpoint. Jain *anekāntavāda* (many-sidedness indexed to explicit standpoints) supplies the exact logical architecture human compatibility requires.

**Tesla's world system.** Unmetered wireless power and decentralized intelligence everywhere complete the material side of the same liberation: essential infrastructure freed from artificial metering and private enclosure.

## 4. Bhagat Singh and Gandhi, held together

In 1931 the two methods stood in open tension: Gandhi's *satyāgraha* insisted on strict nonviolence, while Bhagat Singh's Hindustan Socialist Republican Association had retaliated with arms for Lajpat Rai's killing. Yet the decisive acts traced here are the exact ground where Bhagat Singh and Gandhi converge:
- speech made public at personal cost;
- arrest and institutional retaliation met openly rather than evaded;
- the courtroom turned into a public platform;
- the fast undertaken on one's own body as speech;
- truth held as the one weapon no authority can confiscate.

Bhagat Singh's own Delhi court statement drew that line explicitly ("It is not the cult of the bomb and the pistol"), and hyperactivism operates wholly through those shared instruments: the primary record, the machine-checked warrant, the court, the informed vote, the open public web, and work given freely as a public commons.

Holding Bhagat Singh and Gandhi together without collapsing either is *anekāntavāda* in practice. And Bhagat Singh's final essay from his condemned cell — "It is not my vanity, my friends. It is my mode of thinking that has made me an atheist" — is the same epistemic stance once more: settling every question by open rational inquiry rather than deference to authority or accusation of grandiosity. When identity is understood not as a boundary wall around a group but as the space of paths connecting minds across time, a direct path runs from Lahore in 1928 to the hyperactive knowledge commons today.

